LifeCycle
The Class Component Lifecycle (The "Old" Way)
Class components use specific life cycle methods that are automatically called at different points. This model is broken into three main phases.

Mounting (Birth)
This phase occurs when an instance of a component is being created and inserted into the DOM.

constructor(): Called before the component is mounted. It's used for initializing state and binding event handlers.

render(): The only required method. It examines this.props and this.state and returns the JSX to be rendered.

componentDidMount(): Called immediately after the component is rendered and added to the DOM. This is the perfect place to make API calls to fetch data.

Updating (Living)
An update can be caused by changes to props or state. These methods are called when a component is being re-rendered.

render(): Called again to create the updated UI.

componentDidUpdate(): Called immediately after updating occurs. You can perform side effects here, but you should typically wrap them in a condition to avoid infinite loops.

Unmounting (Death)
This phase occurs when a component is being removed from the DOM.

componentWillUnmount(): Called right before a component is unmounted and destroyed. This is the ideal place for cleanup, such as invalidating timers, canceling network requests, or removing event listeners.

2. The Functional Component Lifecycle with Hooks (The Modern Way) 🔄
Functional components use the useEffect hook to handle all the life cycle events that class components managed with different methods. It combines the concepts of componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate, and componentWillUnmount into a single API.

The behavior of useEffect depends on its second argument, the dependency array.

Mounting (componentDidMount)
To run an effect only once after the initial render, provide an empty dependency array [].

JavaScript

import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // This runs only once, after the first render
    console.log('Component has mounted!');
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(setData);
  }, []); // <-- Empty array means it runs only once

  return <div>{data ? data.message : 'Loading...'}</div>;
}
Updating (componentDidUpdate)
To run an effect after the initial render and every time a specific prop or state value changes, include that value in the dependency array.

JavaScript

useEffect(() => {
  // This runs on mount AND every time `someProp` changes
  console.log(`The value of someProp is now ${someProp}`);
}, [someProp]); // <-- Effect depends on someProp
Unmounting (componentWillUnmount)
To run a cleanup function before the component unmounts (or before the effect runs again), return a function from within useEffect.

JavaScript

useEffect(() => {
  const timerId = setInterval(() => {
    console.log('Timer tick');
  }, 1000);

  // Return a cleanup function
  return () => {
    console.log('Cleaning up the timer!');
    clearInterval(timerId); // This stops the timer when the component unmounts
  };
}, []); // <-- Runs once on mount, cleans up on unmount
The useEffect hook provides a more unified and flexible way to manage component side effects and life cycle events, which is why it's the standard in modern React development.
